WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.419
-Action!

00:00:02.419 --> 00:00:04.515
-Last year, "Game of
Thrones" set a world record

00:00:04.515 --> 00:00:07.983
for the most stunt people
on fire in one shot.

00:00:07.983 --> 00:00:09.262
And it looks like it hurt.

00:00:09.262 --> 00:00:10.170
A lot.

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:12.587
Can't Hollywood just use CGI?

00:00:15.055 --> 00:00:17.246
Each year, dozens of movies and TV shows

00:00:17.246 --> 00:00:19.132
feature scenes where stunt performers

00:00:19.132 --> 00:00:21.019
are set on fire.

00:00:21.019 --> 00:00:23.234
It turns out, this is
often done with real fire,

00:00:23.234 --> 00:00:25.203
not visual effects.

00:00:25.203 --> 00:00:27.016
Fire and water are some
of the hardest things

00:00:27.016 --> 00:00:28.844
to create in CGI.

00:00:28.844 --> 00:00:31.914
And stunt performers bring
a unique take to each scene.

00:00:31.914 --> 00:00:34.840
-What you still get from a person

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:36.482
that you won't get from VFX

00:00:36.482 --> 00:00:39.324
is a human performance and their own take

00:00:39.324 --> 00:00:40.358
on something.

00:00:40.358 --> 00:00:42.317
-That's stunt coordinator Rowley Irlam.

00:00:42.317 --> 00:00:44.578
He's worked on films like
"Guardians of the Galaxy"

00:00:44.578 --> 00:00:46.623
and "Harry Potter and
the Deathly Hallows."

00:00:46.623 --> 00:00:48.603
His latest project has
been the last four seasons

00:00:48.603 --> 00:00:50.295
of "Game of Thrones."

00:00:50.295 --> 00:00:51.666
Sounds easy, right?

00:00:51.666 --> 00:00:53.548
Get a few stunt people, some fire,

00:00:53.548 --> 00:00:55.163
and your scene will look great!

00:00:55.163 --> 00:00:57.594
Turns out, even just a few seconds of fire

00:00:57.594 --> 00:00:59.528
requires a lot of work.

00:00:59.528 --> 00:01:01.051
You're not just setting people on fire,

00:01:01.051 --> 00:01:04.051
you have to set them on fire safely.

00:01:05.361 --> 00:01:07.153
The most extreme type of burn is one

00:01:07.153 --> 00:01:09.640
where the stunt person is
fully engulfed in flame.

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:12.816
But the biggest risk
isn't burning their skin.

00:01:12.816 --> 00:01:14.591
-You need to be holding your breath,

00:01:14.591 --> 00:01:16.775
so you don't breathe in any kind of flame.

00:01:16.775 --> 00:01:18.559
-In order to keep stunt performers safe,

00:01:18.559 --> 00:01:20.258
Rowley and his team count out loud

00:01:20.258 --> 00:01:23.602
to let them know how much time is left.

00:01:23.602 --> 00:01:27.115
-Rowley: 11! 12! Out, out, out!

00:01:27.115 --> 00:01:28.100
-They remain on fire

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:30.109
for no more than 15 seconds.

00:01:30.109 --> 00:01:32.064
-The danger is disorientation,

00:01:32.064 --> 00:01:34.041
or if you were to injure yourself

00:01:34.041 --> 00:01:36.149
or bang into something, you know.

00:01:36.149 --> 00:01:39.238
What you don't want to do is breathe in

00:01:39.238 --> 00:01:42.493
because of a shock of pain or injury.

00:01:42.493 --> 00:01:44.269
-Stunt coordinators try to anticipate

00:01:44.269 --> 00:01:46.130
and eliminate anything that could go wrong

00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:47.420
or cause an injury.

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:49.908
Everything is rehearsed multiple times,

00:01:49.908 --> 00:01:52.595
so everyone knows where
to go and what to do.

00:01:52.595 --> 00:01:53.952
-If you do enough of it,

00:01:53.952 --> 00:01:55.876
you become competent and confident.

00:01:55.876 --> 00:01:58.455
We run a very controlled set, you know,

00:01:58.455 --> 00:02:00.484
we make a plan, and we stick to the plan,

00:02:00.484 --> 00:02:02.535
and we execute the plan.

00:02:02.535 --> 00:02:04.190
-In addition to lots of rehearsal,

00:02:04.190 --> 00:02:06.508
stunt performers wear a
ton of protective gear.

00:02:06.508 --> 00:02:08.071
And their secret ingredient

00:02:08.071 --> 00:02:11.139
is a flame-retardant
material called Zel Jel.

00:02:11.139 --> 00:02:13.487
For "Game of Thrones,"
stunt performers wear

00:02:13.487 --> 00:02:15.766
three layers of fireproof underwear,

00:02:15.766 --> 00:02:17.798
which is soaked in Zel Jel.

00:02:17.798 --> 00:02:20.543
Above that is a rain
suit to prevent the gel

00:02:20.543 --> 00:02:22.761
from being absorbed by the other layers.

00:02:22.761 --> 00:02:25.555
Next is a fire suit
and a thin cotton suit.

00:02:25.555 --> 00:02:28.807
Any exposed skin is
also coated in Zel Jel.

00:02:28.807 --> 00:02:30.875
Finally, their costume goes on top.

00:02:30.875 --> 00:02:33.782
-Rowley: You are now
ready to be set on fire.

00:02:33.782 --> 00:02:35.566
-And Rowley Irlam is no stranger

00:02:35.566 --> 00:02:37.490
to setting people on fire.

00:02:37.490 --> 00:02:39.446
For season seven of "Game of Thrones,"

00:02:39.446 --> 00:02:42.765
20 stunt performers were
set ablaze at a single time,

00:02:42.765 --> 00:02:44.293
and 73 were lit on fire

00:02:44.293 --> 00:02:46.066
throughout the course of the scene.

00:02:46.066 --> 00:02:47.976
Each performer has their
own safety partner,

00:02:47.976 --> 00:02:51.039
who helps them get dressed
and extinguishes their fire.

00:02:51.039 --> 00:02:52.476
For every four safety people,

00:02:52.476 --> 00:02:54.275
there is an additional safety person.

00:02:54.275 --> 00:02:59.275
That's more than 45 crew
members for just one stunt.

00:02:59.323 --> 00:03:00.867
It took weeks of pre-production

00:03:00.867 --> 00:03:02.615
and multiple cameras to shoot.

00:03:02.615 --> 00:03:04.728
But the key to making
that scene convincing

00:03:04.728 --> 00:03:05.929
wasn't the fire.

00:03:05.929 --> 00:03:07.186
It was the acting.

00:03:07.186 --> 00:03:09.576
-What we wanted to do is find the humanity

00:03:09.576 --> 00:03:11.148
of the situation.

00:03:11.148 --> 00:03:12.664
These are the human moments

00:03:12.664 --> 00:03:14.531
when you feel their suffering.

00:03:14.531 --> 00:03:15.985
-Crafting a scene that looks realistic

00:03:15.985 --> 00:03:19.030
while maintaining strict
safety standards isn't easy.

00:03:19.030 --> 00:03:20.412
-Well, the challenging thing is

00:03:20.412 --> 00:03:22.203
to make sure that you
don't make a mistake.

00:03:22.203 --> 00:03:23.951
If we can get through
stuff and make it look

00:03:23.951 --> 00:03:25.599
really, really exciting and dynamic

00:03:25.599 --> 00:03:28.271
and not hurt anybody, then we've won.

00:03:28.271 --> 00:03:30.758
When we were planning
to do the 20-man burn,

00:03:30.758 --> 00:03:32.353
it was going to rain on Monday.

00:03:32.353 --> 00:03:34.225
And I was like, "Well, it's sunny now."

00:03:34.225 --> 00:03:35.662
And they were like,
"Okay, what do you mean?"

00:03:35.662 --> 00:03:37.588
I said, "Well, let's do
this afternoon then."

00:03:37.588 --> 00:03:38.730
And he was like, "But we can't!"

00:03:38.730 --> 00:03:39.689
And I was like, "Well, why?"

00:03:39.689 --> 00:03:41.209
And he went, "I don't know."

